1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
It's an industry Paul Bunyan could have never imagined.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:15,000
Teaming He-Man and high-tech, saws and software, blades and boards,

3
00:00:15,001 --> 00:00:20,000
each cry of timber evokes memories of a wild and woolly past.

4
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
And each tree that falls fuels debate about its future.

5
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, Logging Tech on Modern Marbles.

6
00:00:50,611 --> 00:00:53,560
The roar of machinery and crackling of timber

7
00:00:53,561 --> 00:00:55,560
scream it louder than any statistic.

8
00:00:55,940 --> 00:00:59,220
The world has a ravenous appetite for wood.

9
00:00:59,221 --> 00:01:02,060
Each year, loggers topple 4 billion trees

10
00:01:02,160 --> 00:01:04,060
to satisfy our insatiable demand.

11
00:01:07,560 --> 00:01:11,260
We devour trees for lumber, paper, and byproducts

12
00:01:11,360 --> 00:01:14,560
from aspirin to film to toothpaste.

13
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:18,880
In the last 50 years, global demand has doubled,

14
00:01:18,980 --> 00:01:23,080
and Americans lead the way, consuming 40% of the forest's

15
00:01:23,180 --> 00:01:25,680
resources.

16
00:01:25,780 --> 00:01:29,220
Trying to answer the challenge is a new generation of loggers.

17
00:01:29,221 --> 00:01:31,220
I've grown up in the woods really.

18
00:01:31,320 --> 00:01:37,220
I've just spent many, many days in the woods with my dad and my grandfather.

19
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:41,220
My grandfather had to be physically tough to go out every day and deal with the dangers

20
00:01:41,320 --> 00:01:46,220
of following large timber and getting the production he had to get.

21
00:01:46,320 --> 00:01:53,220
Today, equipment makes our job out here relatively easy, but I would hope that my grandfather

22
00:01:53,320 --> 00:01:58,220
would be as proud of the job that I do today as I am of what he did in his era.

23
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:04,220
The logging industry is characterized by people I would

24
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:08,320
call geniuses with calloused hands, because they had to use

25
00:02:08,420 --> 00:02:11,240
the muscle that they had in their bodies to be able to

26
00:02:11,340 --> 00:02:15,940
invent ways to move logs that may weigh thousands and even

27
00:02:16,040 --> 00:02:20,480
tens of thousands of pounds around in the forest.

28
00:02:20,580 --> 00:02:24,440
A single tree's journey from stump to sawmill seems to be

29
00:02:24,540 --> 00:02:26,280
a model of efficiency.

30
00:02:26,281 --> 00:02:28,680
seems to be a model of efficiency.

31
00:02:28,780 --> 00:02:32,020
It begins not in the forest, but at a computer.

32
00:02:32,120 --> 00:02:35,020
Specialized software helps foresters target groves

33
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:37,220
to be harvested.

34
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:40,200
Basically, the software has a database in it

35
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:42,300
in which people have actually gone out to the woods

36
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:45,400
and collected information about the stands of trees,

37
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:49,040
like the age of them, their species, or the trees per acre

38
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:50,200
in the stand.

39
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:53,040
And you can say, I'm interested in all stands of trees that

40
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:54,580
are 60 years old.

41
00:02:54,581 --> 00:02:56,980
And it'll go through and bring up all of those stands for you

42
00:02:57,080 --> 00:02:59,160
so you can make the decisions about which stands you want

43
00:02:59,260 --> 00:03:03,960
to harvest based on what the computer's pulled up for you.

44
00:03:04,060 --> 00:03:06,800
A tree is quick work for the tool that revolutionized

45
00:03:06,900 --> 00:03:09,700
the industry in the 20th century, the chainsaw.

46
00:03:13,760 --> 00:03:16,400
Side to hell!

47
00:03:16,500 --> 00:03:18,740
Trees that once took several minutes to fell

48
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:20,080
now topple in seconds.

49
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:26,740
But its productivity pales in comparison

50
00:03:26,741 --> 00:03:31,720
with this mechanical titan, the single grip harvester.

51
00:03:31,721 --> 00:03:34,820
Operators maneuver a powerful articulated arm tipped

52
00:03:34,821 --> 00:03:37,540
with a rotating head.

53
00:03:37,541 --> 00:03:40,240
Vice-like grapples take hold of the tree.

54
00:03:40,241 --> 00:03:42,420
Then a chainsaw shears it off at the base.

55
00:03:47,280 --> 00:03:51,980
It then propels the tree through scraping blades that delimit.

56
00:03:51,981 --> 00:03:55,480
At the same time, it carves it into precisely sawn logs,

57
00:03:55,481 --> 00:03:56,880
a process called bucking.

58
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:06,920
The actual grapples that grab the tree are also knives.

59
00:04:06,921 --> 00:04:10,600
And then on the head, there's two wheels that actually suck

60
00:04:10,601 --> 00:04:12,660
the tree through the head.

61
00:04:12,661 --> 00:04:15,740
It'd be similar as taking a stick of straw

62
00:04:15,741 --> 00:04:18,240
with the seed still on top of it and just pulling it

63
00:04:18,241 --> 00:04:19,480
through your fingers.

64
00:04:19,481 --> 00:04:23,720
It works on the same concept as that.

65
00:04:23,820 --> 00:04:26,280
The saw blade is so powerful that few operators

66
00:04:26,380 --> 00:04:28,280
could control it by hand.

67
00:04:28,380 --> 00:04:31,220
Conventional chainsaws have teeth on every other link

68
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:33,520
in the chain.

69
00:04:33,620 --> 00:04:36,020
A harvester's blade has a tooth on every link.

70
00:04:38,940 --> 00:04:41,340
This enables it to saw more rapidly through even

71
00:04:41,440 --> 00:04:43,740
the hardest wood.

72
00:04:43,840 --> 00:04:45,980
With the mechanized harvesting operations,

73
00:04:46,080 --> 00:04:49,480
we can cut down trees 10 times faster than we can with a chainsaw.

74
00:04:49,481 --> 00:04:52,520
And we might cut 70 trees in a day with a chainsaw.

75
00:04:52,620 --> 00:04:57,520
And we might cut 700 with a mechanized harvesting machine.

76
00:04:57,620 --> 00:05:04,520
Most I've ever done in one day is 1,250 trees.

77
00:05:04,620 --> 00:05:07,940
And my record per hour is 172 trees.

78
00:05:10,840 --> 00:05:13,680
Felling and bucking timber is one thing.

79
00:05:13,780 --> 00:05:15,540
Moving is another.

80
00:05:15,640 --> 00:05:18,680
When the terrain permits, vehicles called forwarders

81
00:05:18,681 --> 00:05:24,680
to waiting transport trucks. In more rugged country,

82
00:05:24,780 --> 00:05:30,220
vehicles simply drag the logs. Loggers call it skidding.

83
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:34,220
A variation, high-lead skidding, elevates the front end of a log

84
00:05:34,320 --> 00:05:40,140
from a suspended cable. It begins and ends with crewmen,

85
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:43,140
called choker setters. Choker setting is the process

86
00:05:43,240 --> 00:05:48,040
of hooking the steel cables that yard logs to the landing.

87
00:05:48,041 --> 00:05:51,740
And that choker is basically a steel noose that you put

88
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:52,780
around the log.

89
00:05:52,880 --> 00:05:54,440
And as it tightens up, it squeezes the log

90
00:05:54,540 --> 00:05:58,280
and is how it is yarded or pulled to the landing.

91
00:05:58,380 --> 00:06:02,780
The process is as perilous as it is effective.

92
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:06,320
People working as choker setters today

93
00:06:06,420 --> 00:06:08,900
are faced with a variety of hazards.

94
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:10,600
Cables could break.

95
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:11,700
Logs could fall.

96
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:13,800
Limbs could come out of the trees

97
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:15,800
that were broken by the movement of the log

98
00:06:15,801 --> 00:06:17,800
up the cable corridor.

99
00:06:17,900 --> 00:06:22,040
Just the movement on that steep terrain is difficult.

100
00:06:22,140 --> 00:06:24,040
The communication is crucial.

101
00:06:24,140 --> 00:06:27,280
The lines don't move until a person with a whistle

102
00:06:27,380 --> 00:06:32,320
gets into a position in the clear and provides the signal

103
00:06:32,420 --> 00:06:36,420
to the yarder operator to move the lines.

104
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:42,320
The worst situation you could be in,

105
00:06:42,321 --> 00:06:46,560
and you're not expecting the lines to move, and they do,

106
00:06:46,660 --> 00:06:48,920
and you're in a position where you're going to be in the way.

107
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:52,660
You're talking about logs that weigh tens of thousands of pounds.

108
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:54,700
There are no odds on that.

109
00:06:55,700 --> 00:06:57,220
It's you lose.

110
00:06:57,960 --> 00:07:00,740
Today's men get no break.

111
00:07:01,480 --> 00:07:06,400
Things are moving so fast that they're constantly on the run.

112
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:09,360
The machinery's faster, the rigging's faster.

113
00:07:09,361 --> 00:07:13,860
So today's logging inherently is more dangerous,

114
00:07:13,960 --> 00:07:16,860
except we're not allowing it to be.

115
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:20,480
We're saying safety has to be first.

116
00:07:20,580 --> 00:07:22,300
One of logging's latest innovations

117
00:07:22,400 --> 00:07:24,700
is a radio-controlled choker.

118
00:07:24,800 --> 00:07:26,640
It relieves crewmen of the perilous task

119
00:07:26,740 --> 00:07:29,520
of releasing the nooses.

120
00:07:29,620 --> 00:07:31,640
Releasing them manually is dangerous,

121
00:07:31,740 --> 00:07:34,380
because it is at that moment when logs are most likely

122
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:37,380
to suddenly shift or fall.

123
00:07:37,381 --> 00:07:42,660
Inside this choker bell, there's a battery, electric motor, a receiver, and a circuit board.

124
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:46,620
The way the choker setter sets it is you'll notice there's a button here.

125
00:07:47,540 --> 00:07:54,160
He pushes this button out like that, takes his choker, puts it around the log,

126
00:07:54,700 --> 00:07:57,820
into that slot, and closes that bell.

127
00:07:58,360 --> 00:07:59,720
Now it's locked into position.

128
00:08:01,120 --> 00:08:03,080
So the log will come into the landing.

129
00:08:03,081 --> 00:08:04,060
They'll let it down.

130
00:08:04,061 --> 00:08:06,700
the operator would push a button,

131
00:08:06,800 --> 00:08:09,800
and it would release this choker.

132
00:08:09,900 --> 00:08:12,100
No man has to get into that danger zone.

133
00:08:15,100 --> 00:08:16,800
Logging's cutting-edge technology

134
00:08:16,900 --> 00:08:19,200
takes its most literal form in the sawmill.

135
00:08:22,820 --> 00:08:25,220
Georgia Pacific's mill in Coos Bay, Oregon,

136
00:08:25,320 --> 00:08:27,760
built in 1994, is one of the most productive

137
00:08:27,860 --> 00:08:30,160
in the United States.

138
00:08:30,260 --> 00:08:32,780
It takes about 100 log trucks a day

139
00:08:32,781 --> 00:08:36,380
to keep up with the sawmill. Non-annual basis for producing

140
00:08:36,381 --> 00:08:38,780
about 200 million feet of lumber.

141
00:08:38,781 --> 00:08:42,560
That's enough to build about 17,000 average single-family

142
00:08:42,561 --> 00:08:44,600
homes. Or if you want to look at another

143
00:08:44,601 --> 00:08:47,900
way, enough lumber to make a 2x4 that would stretch around the

144
00:08:47,901 --> 00:08:55,500
world two times. A log's first encounter here

145
00:08:55,501 --> 00:08:58,500
is with the debarker. The bark must be scraped off

146
00:08:58,501 --> 00:09:01,680
because it is encased with dirt that can dull the saws that lie

147
00:09:01,681 --> 00:09:05,680
that lie ahead.

148
00:09:05,780 --> 00:09:07,980
DeBarked logs then race by conveyor

149
00:09:08,080 --> 00:09:10,680
before the vigilant eyes of the Sawyer.

150
00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:13,820
He mans a computerized scanner that has only a fleeting instant

151
00:09:13,920 --> 00:09:16,020
to formulate the most efficient cutting strategy

152
00:09:16,120 --> 00:09:20,140
for each piece of timber.

153
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:21,940
On an average day in our sawmill,

154
00:09:22,040 --> 00:09:25,340
we're going to run 4,000 logs through the head rig,

155
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:27,680
or in this case, what we call the chip and saw.

156
00:09:27,780 --> 00:09:29,680
To have a person try and make a decision

157
00:09:29,681 --> 00:09:33,520
and get the most value out of that log 4,000 times a day

158
00:09:33,521 --> 00:09:35,460
without making a mistake, it just doesn't work.

159
00:09:36,220 --> 00:09:40,880
So today's modern sawmill uses a lot of computers, a lot of scanners.

160
00:09:42,580 --> 00:09:44,960
As the log comes through here, I'm looking at it,

161
00:09:45,220 --> 00:09:47,420
and I'm looking at the scan that the computer's given.

162
00:09:47,900 --> 00:09:50,860
It can see everything, knots, taper,

163
00:09:51,320 --> 00:09:54,720
and then it basically immediately, as soon as it clears the scanner,

164
00:09:54,800 --> 00:09:57,200
it's made a decision, and it pops up a picture,

165
00:09:57,201 --> 00:10:00,700
And it gives the length, the diameter, every board that's

166
00:10:00,800 --> 00:10:01,800
in there.

167
00:10:01,900 --> 00:10:06,640
And usually, the scanner does a really good solution.

168
00:10:06,740 --> 00:10:09,940
Two kinds of blades transform logs into lumber.

169
00:10:10,040 --> 00:10:13,120
A band saw, a loop of steel strung tight between two

170
00:10:13,220 --> 00:10:16,580
pulleys, squares off logs into more manageable units called

171
00:10:16,680 --> 00:10:19,380
cants.

172
00:10:19,480 --> 00:10:21,420
Down the line, a gang of circular saws

173
00:10:21,520 --> 00:10:24,600
carves the cants in one pass into separate boards.

174
00:10:27,200 --> 00:10:34,200
The blades are braised with carbide, a hard carbon compound

175
00:10:34,201 --> 00:10:37,200
that helps the teeth stay sharper longer.

176
00:10:37,201 --> 00:10:42,200
But the real key to a saw's efficiency is its thickness, or

177
00:10:42,201 --> 00:10:46,200
kerf. Thin blades produce the least sawdust.

178
00:10:46,201 --> 00:10:50,200
20 years ago, 30 years ago, saw kerf, which is the thickness of

179
00:10:50,201 --> 00:10:54,200
the saw, could have been as much as a quarter of an inch, or

180
00:10:54,201 --> 00:10:58,200
or 250 thousandths, that's the same as a quarter of an inch.

181
00:10:58,201 --> 00:11:02,200
Today, I can't tell you specifics because it's kind of one of our secrets

182
00:11:02,201 --> 00:11:05,200
that we're significantly less than half of that.

183
00:11:05,201 --> 00:11:10,200
If you were to use the old circle saws, you might get 10 pieces of lumber out of it.

184
00:11:10,201 --> 00:11:14,200
Now with the technology and the improvements, you may get 13 or 14 pieces of lumber out of it.

185
00:11:14,201 --> 00:11:19,200
You've got that much more of that log going into lumber instead of sawdust.

186
00:11:19,201 --> 00:11:24,200
Cut lumber finally makes its way to a crew of graders who

187
00:11:24,300 --> 00:11:26,700
inspect each piece to determine its quality.

188
00:11:26,800 --> 00:11:29,440
They have just a second, maybe a second and a half,

189
00:11:29,540 --> 00:11:33,140
to look at each piece and apply the grade to that piece

190
00:11:33,240 --> 00:11:34,740
according to the characteristics that they're

191
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:36,660
viewing at the time.

192
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:39,920
We have a special crayon that the graders use that are

193
00:11:40,020 --> 00:11:41,460
readable by this system.

194
00:11:41,560 --> 00:11:43,560
As it passes underneath the camera lens,

195
00:11:43,660 --> 00:11:46,520
it takes a photograph of that, and it will be

196
00:11:46,521 --> 00:11:49,820
It takes a photograph of that, and it will sort the lumber

197
00:11:49,920 --> 00:11:52,420
out based on the mark that graders have put on the lumber.

198
00:11:55,860 --> 00:11:58,360
Lumber bound for market is used to make everything

199
00:11:58,460 --> 00:12:01,960
from furniture to crates to toys.

200
00:12:02,060 --> 00:12:04,480
But most of it ends up here.

201
00:12:04,580 --> 00:12:07,080
The construction industry consumes every other board

202
00:12:07,180 --> 00:12:10,280
produced in the United States.

203
00:12:10,380 --> 00:12:12,820
A nation with a voracious appetite for wood

204
00:12:12,920 --> 00:12:13,920
is using it to grow.

205
00:12:16,520 --> 00:12:19,320
Feeding that appetite is a challenge as old as America

206
00:12:19,420 --> 00:12:20,820
itself.

207
00:12:20,920 --> 00:12:24,920
The hardy men who started it all forged a legend as large as the

208
00:12:25,020 --> 00:12:26,260
timber they felled.

209
00:12:29,360 --> 00:12:32,060
Wood abounds in medicine cabinets.

210
00:12:32,160 --> 00:12:35,780
Pills in tablet form are held together with lignin, a glue

211
00:12:35,880 --> 00:12:37,780
extracted from trees.

212
00:12:37,880 --> 00:12:40,480
Adhesive on bandages is made from sap.

213
00:12:40,580 --> 00:12:44,180
Toothpaste owes its consistency to cellulose, a natural wood

214
00:12:44,280 --> 00:12:46,520
polymer.

215
00:12:46,521 --> 00:12:53,520
3, 2, 1, go!

216
00:12:53,521 --> 00:12:57,520
Just as the rodeo pays homage to the romantic image

217
00:12:57,521 --> 00:13:01,520
of the cowboy, timber sports celebrate the larger-than-life

218
00:13:01,521 --> 00:13:04,520
persona of the lumberjack.

219
00:13:04,521 --> 00:13:08,520
Although 21st century loggers embrace the advantages of

220
00:13:08,521 --> 00:13:13,520
mechanization, they revel in their roots.

221
00:13:13,521 --> 00:13:17,420
Those roots date back to the beginning of the last ice age.

222
00:13:17,520 --> 00:13:20,420
In a moment of inspiration, an ancient innovator sought to

223
00:13:20,520 --> 00:13:24,260
improve the hand-held sharpened stone he used as a cutting tool.

224
00:13:27,060 --> 00:13:30,880
He attached it to a long wooden handle, creating logging's most

225
00:13:30,980 --> 00:13:37,380
time-honored tool, the axe. In the centuries ahead, bronze

226
00:13:37,480 --> 00:13:41,420
replaced stone, eventually empowering the Egyptians to

227
00:13:41,421 --> 00:13:45,920
Egyptians to complete history's oldest known logging expedition.

228
00:13:46,020 --> 00:13:50,320
In 2700 BC, they harvested the lush cedar forests that covered

229
00:13:50,420 --> 00:13:55,060
much of Lebanon. Today, only a few scattered cedars

230
00:13:55,160 --> 00:13:59,580
remain there. Tools and techniques

231
00:13:59,680 --> 00:14:02,480
advanced little in the centuries that followed.

232
00:14:02,580 --> 00:14:06,180
In every culture, the essentials were the same, an axe, a strong

233
00:14:06,181 --> 00:14:10,680
back and a river to transport the heavy timber.

234
00:14:10,780 --> 00:14:13,680
For the next two millennia, loggers feasted on the timber

235
00:14:13,780 --> 00:14:16,020
lands, especially in Europe.

236
00:14:16,120 --> 00:14:19,520
But until the 14th century, woodworkers sawed logs

237
00:14:19,620 --> 00:14:23,340
strictly by hand. Then water wheels began

238
00:14:23,440 --> 00:14:26,840
powering the first sawmills in Germany, followed by windmill

239
00:14:26,940 --> 00:14:32,080
powered systems in Holland. Forests fell ever faster as the

240
00:14:32,081 --> 00:14:35,920
as the population rose. Today, only 3% of Europe's

241
00:14:35,921 --> 00:14:41,420
ancient woodlands remain. In the New World, old techniques

242
00:14:41,421 --> 00:14:44,420
enabled lumberjacks to successfully assail the forests of

243
00:14:44,421 --> 00:14:47,820
New England and the Great Lakes. But when loggers ventured

244
00:14:47,821 --> 00:14:51,340
west in the 1850s, time-honored methods proved woefully

245
00:14:51,341 --> 00:14:54,340
inadequate. They were thunderstruck as they

246
00:14:54,341 --> 00:14:59,040
stepped in the shadows of the largest trees on the planet.

247
00:14:59,041 --> 00:15:02,240
Douglas fir, Sitka spruce, coast redwoods,

248
00:15:02,340 --> 00:15:06,840
and giant sequoias dwarf the white pines abundant in the east.

249
00:15:06,940 --> 00:15:09,180
The attitude of the logger who came out here

250
00:15:09,280 --> 00:15:10,480
was one of all.

251
00:15:10,580 --> 00:15:13,280
The average tree in the forests of Maine was about a big tree

252
00:15:13,380 --> 00:15:16,420
was 100 feet high, maybe two or three feet thick.

253
00:15:16,520 --> 00:15:17,800
When they got out here, they found,

254
00:15:17,900 --> 00:15:19,520
in the case of the giant sequoias,

255
00:15:19,620 --> 00:15:23,160
they were looking at a tree 230 feet high in some instances,

256
00:15:23,260 --> 00:15:24,560
30 feet thick.

257
00:15:24,660 --> 00:15:26,560
So when the loggers came west, they

258
00:15:26,660 --> 00:15:29,040
had no idea what they were getting into.

259
00:15:29,041 --> 00:15:33,240
Imagine somebody coming up to a tree 30 feet across and 230 feet

260
00:15:33,340 --> 00:15:36,640
high with a handsaw and an axe.

261
00:15:36,740 --> 00:15:39,080
Lumberjacks cast aside their six-foot saws

262
00:15:39,180 --> 00:15:42,980
and forged models up to 22 feet long.

263
00:15:43,080 --> 00:15:45,180
The men trying to harvest the treasure of timber

264
00:15:45,280 --> 00:15:48,500
were a motley throng of wayward settlers, ship deserters,

265
00:15:48,600 --> 00:15:50,800
and frustrated 49ers.

266
00:15:50,900 --> 00:15:54,140
But these misfits became bonded by a common purpose,

267
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:56,400
a lust for adventure and a freewheeling life

268
00:15:56,401 --> 00:15:58,900
in one of nature's grandest arenas.

269
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:00,440
They were a pretty hardy bunch.

270
00:16:00,540 --> 00:16:02,340
You know, they were willing to fight at the drop of a hat.

271
00:16:02,440 --> 00:16:04,940
And of course, they were in marvelous shape, lean,

272
00:16:05,040 --> 00:16:06,140
and not too big.

273
00:16:06,240 --> 00:16:07,640
The average guy who worked in the woods

274
00:16:07,740 --> 00:16:10,840
was about 5'7 or 8, maybe 150 pounds.

275
00:16:10,940 --> 00:16:14,260
Once in a while, you'd find a big guy, 6'4, 220.

276
00:16:14,360 --> 00:16:18,260
But on the whole, they were a pretty lean, wiry group.

277
00:16:18,360 --> 00:16:19,860
One of the first obstacles loggers

278
00:16:19,960 --> 00:16:22,560
faced was the nearly impenetrable swollen bases

279
00:16:22,660 --> 00:16:24,800
of the mammoth trees.

280
00:16:24,801 --> 00:16:27,200
All the resins from years and years

281
00:16:27,300 --> 00:16:30,540
start to congregate in the last six, eight feet

282
00:16:30,640 --> 00:16:32,940
of the trunk of the tree right above the stump.

283
00:16:33,040 --> 00:16:37,740
And so what they did is they had to get above that butt swell.

284
00:16:37,840 --> 00:16:40,040
The solution, called the springboard,

285
00:16:40,140 --> 00:16:42,460
became standard practice among lumberjacks well

286
00:16:42,560 --> 00:16:43,960
into the 20th century.

287
00:16:44,060 --> 00:16:47,160
Springboard is really nothing more than about a six

288
00:16:47,260 --> 00:16:48,860
or eight inch wide board.

289
00:16:48,960 --> 00:16:52,000
It had a little hook on the end, and that would catch.

290
00:16:52,001 --> 00:16:54,900
And you could hop up, and you could put a 250-pound man

291
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,740
on this springboard.

292
00:16:57,840 --> 00:17:00,580
Springboards gave lumberjacks just the boost they needed

293
00:17:00,680 --> 00:17:02,640
to begin the laborious process of bringing

294
00:17:02,740 --> 00:17:05,240
the mighty trees down.

295
00:17:05,340 --> 00:17:07,720
They would come in and decide where their horizontal

296
00:17:07,820 --> 00:17:09,220
undercut was going to be.

297
00:17:09,320 --> 00:17:12,720
And then they would cut across the tree to a depth maybe about

298
00:17:12,820 --> 00:17:16,560
a third or so of the tree diameter.

299
00:17:16,660 --> 00:17:19,760
They would then come in and chop out the undercut.

300
00:17:19,761 --> 00:17:30,160
After the tree is faced up, we call it, they would come around behind the tree and then begin sawing at another horizontal cut, a little bit above the undercut.

301
00:17:30,161 --> 00:17:39,020
And then they'll have to leave a hinge between the front cut or the undercut and the back cut to guide the tree in the direction that they want it to go.

302
00:17:39,021 --> 00:17:44,020
The lumberjacks would even put out stakes or markers out

303
00:17:44,120 --> 00:17:46,500
in the area where they were going to drop the tree,

304
00:17:46,600 --> 00:17:49,060
and they'd take bets on whether or not they could drive a stake

305
00:17:49,160 --> 00:17:50,440
in the ground with a tree.

306
00:17:50,540 --> 00:17:53,060
As the trees fell, their titanic limbs

307
00:17:53,160 --> 00:17:55,600
often snapped off and lodged in the treetops towering

308
00:17:55,700 --> 00:17:57,000
above the forest floor.

309
00:17:57,100 --> 00:17:59,580
Days, even weeks later, they could plummet

310
00:17:59,680 --> 00:18:01,440
toward unsuspecting loggers.

311
00:18:01,540 --> 00:18:04,980
Well, if limbs broke out, and especially the limb from, say,

312
00:18:05,080 --> 00:18:07,020
100 feet away from the tree, then they would

313
00:18:07,021 --> 00:18:09,220
especially the limb from, say, 100 feet up,

314
00:18:09,320 --> 00:18:12,920
and came hustling down, that's what we called a widow maker.

315
00:18:13,020 --> 00:18:15,120
Because that fell, and if that hit him on the head,

316
00:18:15,220 --> 00:18:17,660
I mean, it would just drive him in the ground.

317
00:18:17,760 --> 00:18:20,060
And if you were a married lumberjack,

318
00:18:20,160 --> 00:18:24,480
you've just, your widow is going to a funeral now.

319
00:18:24,580 --> 00:18:26,480
Every tree that toppled set in motion

320
00:18:26,580 --> 00:18:29,080
the lumberjack's colorful supporting cast,

321
00:18:29,180 --> 00:18:34,180
assigned the Herculean task of transporting the huge logs.

322
00:18:34,280 --> 00:18:36,580
Their destination was a nearby river.

323
00:18:36,581 --> 00:18:40,080
the critical link to civilization around which every logging camp

324
00:18:40,180 --> 00:18:45,180
was based. First came the Bullwhackers.

325
00:18:45,280 --> 00:18:49,420
For decades, their teams of oxen dragged timber by steel chains

326
00:18:49,520 --> 00:18:55,540
over a path of half-buried logs called the Skid Road.

327
00:18:55,640 --> 00:18:59,540
At the end of the Skid Road were the sure-footed river pigs.

328
00:18:59,640 --> 00:19:02,940
For the next century, they navigated America's waterways,

329
00:19:02,941 --> 00:19:07,520
driving logs as far as 70 miles to the sawmills.

330
00:19:07,620 --> 00:19:10,240
Each came equipped only with spiked boots and a pole

331
00:19:10,340 --> 00:19:14,820
fitted with a hook designed to maneuver the floating timber.

332
00:19:14,920 --> 00:19:17,180
Fighting a constant battle to break up log jams

333
00:19:17,280 --> 00:19:19,700
in the congested rivers, every river pig

334
00:19:19,800 --> 00:19:23,420
knew that one misstep could be fatal.

335
00:19:23,520 --> 00:19:25,960
Imagine trying to stand on a log

336
00:19:26,060 --> 00:19:29,660
or moving from one log to another on a river.

337
00:19:29,760 --> 00:19:32,340
And if you fell off of those logs into the water,

338
00:19:32,341 --> 00:19:34,340
and the water was filled with logs,

339
00:19:34,440 --> 00:19:36,240
he might not come up because he might not

340
00:19:36,340 --> 00:19:38,280
have a place to come up.

341
00:19:38,380 --> 00:19:41,380
I could just imagine that feeling of trying

342
00:19:41,480 --> 00:19:43,420
to find daylight, trying to look up,

343
00:19:43,520 --> 00:19:45,720
and all he could see above you was a wall of logs

344
00:19:45,820 --> 00:19:48,920
and just going, this is not good.

345
00:19:49,020 --> 00:19:51,160
After hours, the river pigs and their colleagues

346
00:19:51,260 --> 00:19:54,760
brought their bravado indoors to the crowded bunkhouses.

347
00:19:54,860 --> 00:19:58,340
Up to 40 shared one room, reeking of sweat, tobacco,

348
00:19:58,341 --> 00:20:05,340
socks and overflowing spittoons. A more popular venue was the camp's

349
00:20:05,341 --> 00:20:09,340
dining hall. The main event for loggers was

350
00:20:09,341 --> 00:20:13,340
food. The average loggers burned 8,000 calories

351
00:20:13,341 --> 00:20:17,340
a day because his work was so hard he never got fat.

352
00:20:17,341 --> 00:20:21,340
The loggers' appetites, however, weren't restricted to food.

353
00:20:21,341 --> 00:20:24,340
Many sought release from their perilous profession in brothels

354
00:20:24,341 --> 00:20:27,540
in brothels and saloons that sprung up like weeds

355
00:20:27,640 --> 00:20:31,340
around their camp. After blowing four months' salary

356
00:20:31,440 --> 00:20:34,380
and one night of debauchery, they returned to the forests

357
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:36,080
to forge their place in history.

358
00:20:39,080 --> 00:20:41,800
They had little inkling that in years to come,

359
00:20:41,900 --> 00:20:44,100
new machines and daring technologies

360
00:20:44,200 --> 00:20:50,100
would transform their world. The term Skid Row takes its name

361
00:20:50,200 --> 00:20:53,100
from the rundown saloons and boarding houses near the Skid

362
00:20:53,101 --> 00:21:03,100
of western logging camps. Beginning in the 1870s,

363
00:21:03,101 --> 00:21:07,100
timber men in the remote western forests heard the shrill echo

364
00:21:07,101 --> 00:21:12,100
of progress. The first transcontinental trains

365
00:21:12,101 --> 00:21:15,100
linked their vast virgin woodlands with eastern markets,

366
00:21:15,101 --> 00:21:20,100
whose own forests had been depleted. Demand soared, but the

367
00:21:20,101 --> 00:21:23,140
but the industrial revolution that drove the railroad west

368
00:21:23,240 --> 00:21:26,580
also empowered the loggers.

369
00:21:26,680 --> 00:21:30,580
Mechanization radically changed how timber was moved.

370
00:21:30,680 --> 00:21:33,580
For decades to come, a huffing monstrosity

371
00:21:33,680 --> 00:21:36,140
called a donkey engine was the nucleus

372
00:21:36,240 --> 00:21:38,260
of every logging operation.

373
00:21:38,360 --> 00:21:40,920
Powered by steam, it turned a capstan

374
00:21:41,020 --> 00:21:43,320
that could pull cable-drawn logs more powerfully

375
00:21:43,420 --> 00:21:44,920
than any animal.

376
00:21:45,020 --> 00:21:49,060
The donkey name came about because it was such a small

377
00:21:49,061 --> 00:21:51,860
it was such a small machine, people said it really didn't

378
00:21:51,861 --> 00:21:54,340
have enough horsepower, so they couldn't call it a horse,

379
00:21:54,341 --> 00:21:56,840
so they named it the donkey instead.

380
00:21:56,841 --> 00:21:59,160
Donkey engines packed enough power, however,

381
00:21:59,161 --> 00:22:02,040
to inspire the high-lead skidding technique still in use

382
00:22:02,041 --> 00:22:04,200
today.

383
00:22:04,201 --> 00:22:07,380
A donkey's tow line was wound over a pulley atop what was

384
00:22:07,381 --> 00:22:09,680
called a spar tree.

385
00:22:09,681 --> 00:22:12,920
The increased leverage enabled it to pull logs up to 2,000

386
00:22:12,921 --> 00:22:16,720
feet away, with their head ends high in the air, riding free

387
00:22:16,721 --> 00:22:19,220
above the stumps and underbrush.

388
00:22:19,221 --> 00:22:21,660
Moving logs when they're totally on the ground

389
00:22:21,661 --> 00:22:24,900
takes a lot more energy than if you have one end of the log

390
00:22:24,901 --> 00:22:25,900
lifted.

391
00:22:25,901 --> 00:22:29,240
Cables were elevated so that you would get at least one end

392
00:22:29,241 --> 00:22:31,760
suspension of the log above the ground

393
00:22:31,761 --> 00:22:33,540
to reduce the amount of energy required

394
00:22:33,541 --> 00:22:36,140
to pull those logs in.

395
00:22:36,141 --> 00:22:40,580
Productivity soared, but so did the accident rate.

396
00:22:40,581 --> 00:22:42,980
One of the biggest hazards was the bite of the line.

397
00:22:42,981 --> 00:22:48,080
It's B-I-G-H-T, not B-I-T-E, the bite of the line.

398
00:22:48,081 --> 00:22:50,720
The donkey engine would start to pull that cable up,

399
00:22:50,721 --> 00:22:51,780
and it would pull tight.

400
00:22:51,781 --> 00:22:55,360
If you were caught in the bite of the line with a foot or an ankle,

401
00:22:55,361 --> 00:22:58,620
that was the end of your legs.

402
00:22:58,621 --> 00:23:01,900
As the new system forced the bullwhackers out of a job,

403
00:23:01,901 --> 00:23:06,100
it created a spectacular new specialist, the high rigger.

404
00:23:06,101 --> 00:23:08,440
Equipped with spiked boots and leg spurs,

405
00:23:08,441 --> 00:23:10,900
he had the demanding task of ascending and prepping

406
00:23:10,901 --> 00:23:12,220
the spar tree.

407
00:23:12,221 --> 00:23:15,920
The high climbers would climb probably 160 to 180 feet

408
00:23:16,020 --> 00:23:18,220
up in the air, although there were some spar trees that

409
00:23:18,320 --> 00:23:20,120
were taller than that.

410
00:23:20,220 --> 00:23:23,060
That's the kind of height that you needed.

411
00:23:23,160 --> 00:23:26,060
At a pre-calculated level, the high rigger

412
00:23:26,160 --> 00:23:28,980
faced the daunting task of cutting off the top of the tree.

413
00:23:33,380 --> 00:23:36,780
And after that tree fell, the weight of that tree

414
00:23:36,880 --> 00:23:40,520
would push that spar tree back, and you would just rock back

415
00:23:40,521 --> 00:23:42,560
and forth.

416
00:23:42,660 --> 00:23:44,220
It started to show off.

417
00:23:44,320 --> 00:23:48,160
He'd go up to the top of the tree and sit and have a cigarette.

418
00:23:48,260 --> 00:23:52,200
And if he really had nerves of steel, he'd do a headstand.

419
00:23:52,300 --> 00:23:54,500
With the spar tree topped off, the high rigor

420
00:23:54,600 --> 00:23:57,300
assisted crewmen in hoisting and securing the high lead

421
00:23:57,400 --> 00:23:59,140
pulley.

422
00:23:59,240 --> 00:24:02,440
As this new breed of daredevils elevated log transport

423
00:24:02,540 --> 00:24:06,120
to productive new heights, locomotive drivers below

424
00:24:06,220 --> 00:24:08,880
were literally spinning their wheels.

425
00:24:08,881 --> 00:24:11,380
For a decade, all efforts to haul logs by rail

426
00:24:11,480 --> 00:24:15,120
in the mountainous timber country failed miserably.

427
00:24:15,220 --> 00:24:17,360
Locomotives had little or no traction

428
00:24:17,460 --> 00:24:19,960
on the steep forest grades.

429
00:24:20,060 --> 00:24:23,260
In 1880, a Michigan lumberman named Ephraim Shea

430
00:24:23,360 --> 00:24:26,540
invented the solution.

431
00:24:26,640 --> 00:24:30,240
The Shea locomotive possessed one radical design change.

432
00:24:30,340 --> 00:24:33,140
Power from the lone cylinder was transmitted to the eight

433
00:24:33,240 --> 00:24:36,740
wheels not by rods, but by gears.

434
00:24:36,741 --> 00:24:38,480
Every wheel was locked in a gear,

435
00:24:38,580 --> 00:24:40,980
so there was no way a wheel could slip.

436
00:24:41,080 --> 00:24:42,880
It's like a four-wheel drive.

437
00:24:42,980 --> 00:24:46,680
Plus, they were lighter, could navigate grades up to 10 or 12,

438
00:24:46,780 --> 00:24:49,080
14%, which was unheard of.

439
00:24:49,180 --> 00:24:53,080
And they could run equally well backward or forward.

440
00:24:53,180 --> 00:24:56,500
Shays enabled loggers to penetrate deeper and deeper

441
00:24:56,600 --> 00:24:58,800
into the Western forests.

442
00:24:58,900 --> 00:25:01,300
Trainloads of timber rolled from regions once thought

443
00:25:01,400 --> 00:25:03,200
inaccessible.

444
00:25:03,300 --> 00:25:06,740
Logs also began moving along the most elaborate and unconventional

445
00:25:06,741 --> 00:25:10,440
the most conventional transport system loggers ever devised,

446
00:25:10,441 --> 00:25:13,280
the flume. It was literally a man-made

447
00:25:13,281 --> 00:25:16,680
river. They built them in 16-foot boxes,

448
00:25:16,681 --> 00:25:20,780
as they called them, sections 16 feet long, and hoisted them up

449
00:25:20,781 --> 00:25:23,300
on top of trestles in that fashion.

450
00:25:23,301 --> 00:25:26,900
As they start down the real steep areas, the lumber might be going

451
00:25:26,901 --> 00:25:29,100
50 miles an hour, splashing everywhere.

452
00:25:29,101 --> 00:25:32,400
Where there was very little drop in elevation, you could walk

453
00:25:32,401 --> 00:25:35,700
along faster than the lumber was floating.

454
00:25:35,701 --> 00:25:37,940
was maintaining the flow of timber along routes

455
00:25:38,040 --> 00:25:40,740
that stretched for miles.

456
00:25:40,840 --> 00:25:43,640
A new crew of specialists, flume herders,

457
00:25:43,740 --> 00:25:47,440
manned remote outposts to guard against leaks and log jams.

458
00:25:47,540 --> 00:25:50,140
The flume herders lived there full time,

459
00:25:50,240 --> 00:25:52,460
and they had a bell hanging out over the flume.

460
00:25:52,560 --> 00:25:55,060
So as the lumber went by, it ticked the bell.

461
00:25:55,160 --> 00:25:56,820
So in the middle of the night, if a guy woke up

462
00:25:56,920 --> 00:25:58,160
and he didn't hear the bell ticking,

463
00:25:58,260 --> 00:25:59,560
he knew there was a problem.

464
00:25:59,660 --> 00:26:02,560
And he'd jump up, trying to find the source of where

465
00:26:02,660 --> 00:26:04,200
the jam hub was.

466
00:26:04,201 --> 00:26:06,800
And if you can imagine, some of these trestles

467
00:26:06,900 --> 00:26:09,100
that the flumes were over as they went over these draws

468
00:26:09,200 --> 00:26:12,500
and gullies were 60 to 70 feet up in the air.

469
00:26:12,600 --> 00:26:16,140
These guys were walking on a little tiny catwalk.

470
00:26:16,240 --> 00:26:18,240
That was an extremely dangerous job,

471
00:26:18,340 --> 00:26:21,960
and there are many occasions where a flume herder fell off

472
00:26:22,060 --> 00:26:25,260
the catwalk to his death.

473
00:26:25,360 --> 00:26:28,460
Beyond the danger, however, was fun.

474
00:26:28,560 --> 00:26:30,960
For years to come, loggers seeking new thrills

475
00:26:31,060 --> 00:26:34,200
found them in makeshift flume boats.

476
00:26:34,201 --> 00:26:38,200
Anyone who's been drenched on an amusement park log ride can

477
00:26:38,201 --> 00:26:43,200
blame it on them. By flume, river, and rail,

478
00:26:43,201 --> 00:26:47,200
logs poured into the sawmills. The industry's survival depended

479
00:26:47,201 --> 00:26:52,200
on the Sawyer's ability to keep pace with the lumberjacks.

480
00:26:52,201 --> 00:26:55,200
Until the 1880s, their most crucial tool was the circular

481
00:26:55,201 --> 00:26:59,200
saw. It was invented in 1777, but now,

482
00:26:59,201 --> 00:27:03,200
coupled with steam, its full potential was being harnessed.

483
00:27:03,201 --> 00:27:07,100
being harnessed. Still, the enormous redwood and

484
00:27:07,101 --> 00:27:12,000
sequoia logs were often more than they could handle.

485
00:27:12,001 --> 00:27:16,340
In the 1870s, after years of persistence, engineers finally

486
00:27:16,341 --> 00:27:19,040
delivered a solution. They developed the first

487
00:27:19,041 --> 00:27:25,660
practical models of an invention patented in 1808, the band saw.

488
00:27:25,661 --> 00:27:29,060
For decades, craftsmen had been unable to accurately and securely

489
00:27:29,061 --> 00:27:32,940
join the ends of the band. Advances in welding techniques

490
00:27:32,941 --> 00:27:35,780
resulted in steel loops that could withstand

491
00:27:35,880 --> 00:27:39,040
the enormous pressure exerted by the flywheels above and below

492
00:27:39,140 --> 00:27:40,480
the carriage.

493
00:27:40,580 --> 00:27:43,580
The fast, thin blades could slice unerringly through even

494
00:27:43,680 --> 00:27:46,180
the most gargantuan logs.

495
00:27:46,280 --> 00:27:49,660
The advancing industry was supplying a rising nation all

496
00:27:49,760 --> 00:27:51,700
the lumber it needed to grow.

497
00:27:51,800 --> 00:27:54,800
But many looked to America's disappearing forests

498
00:27:54,900 --> 00:27:56,900
and worried that the price was too high.

499
00:27:56,901 --> 00:28:02,900
high. California's 54-mile Sanger

500
00:28:02,901 --> 00:28:05,600
Flume was the longest ever constructed.

501
00:28:05,601 --> 00:28:10,540
It was completed in 1890 at a cost of $300,000, equivalent

502
00:28:10,541 --> 00:28:12,540
to nearly $6 million today.

503
00:28:20,360 --> 00:28:23,700
As the 20th century dawned, skyrocketing demand

504
00:28:23,701 --> 00:28:28,940
were transforming logging into a colossus of mechanized industry.

505
00:28:28,941 --> 00:28:32,100
A rough and ready trade was absorbed by corporate America

506
00:28:32,101 --> 00:28:36,340
and the timber barons. The greatest of them all

507
00:28:36,341 --> 00:28:39,640
was Frederick Weyerhaeuser, a German immigrant who began

508
00:28:39,641 --> 00:28:43,060
as a night watchman in an Illinois sawmill.

509
00:28:43,061 --> 00:28:47,560
In 1858, when the mill hit hard times, Weyerhaeuser scraped

510
00:28:47,561 --> 00:28:49,860
together enough money to buy it.

511
00:28:49,861 --> 00:28:53,700
In the decades that followed, he built up the most powerful

512
00:28:53,701 --> 00:28:56,000
company surrounding the Great Lakes.

513
00:28:56,100 --> 00:29:01,000
Then he turned west. In 1900, Warehauser bought

514
00:29:01,100 --> 00:29:05,440
900,000 acres of prime Washington timberland from the

515
00:29:05,540 --> 00:29:08,640
Northern Pacific Railroad. The price tag?

516
00:29:08,740 --> 00:29:12,980
$5.4 million, to that time the largest private land

517
00:29:13,080 --> 00:29:16,560
transaction in American history.

518
00:29:16,660 --> 00:29:19,660
As the Warehauser's of the west took control of the industry,

519
00:29:19,661 --> 00:29:23,360
there was still room for a maverick.

520
00:29:23,460 --> 00:29:26,760
Simon Benson was a former bullwhacker who built his own

521
00:29:26,860 --> 00:29:29,160
thriving company in Washington.

522
00:29:29,260 --> 00:29:34,000
In 1906, the enterprising Benson built a sawmill in San Diego.

523
00:29:34,100 --> 00:29:37,020
His master plan was to satisfy all the lumber needs of a

524
00:29:37,120 --> 00:29:39,520
growing Southern California.

525
00:29:39,620 --> 00:29:41,720
But shipping his logs from Washington by rail

526
00:29:41,820 --> 00:29:44,720
would spell bankruptcy.

527
00:29:44,820 --> 00:29:48,760
Benson's solution was as daring as it was ingenious.

528
00:29:48,761 --> 00:29:52,200
Like a 20th century Noah, he would mass his logs

529
00:29:52,300 --> 00:29:56,100
to construct enormous ocean-going rafts.

530
00:29:56,200 --> 00:29:58,900
What he designed was a timber frame.

531
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:01,300
And this thing was almost 1,000 feet long.

532
00:30:01,400 --> 00:30:03,240
And as the logs came in from the woods,

533
00:30:03,340 --> 00:30:07,020
he would take this big derrick and it would load these logs

534
00:30:07,120 --> 00:30:08,520
into this wooden frame.

535
00:30:08,620 --> 00:30:11,720
And he was very, very careful of overlapping the joints

536
00:30:11,820 --> 00:30:15,260
of the logs so he didn't have all the ends at one place.

537
00:30:15,360 --> 00:30:17,820
He'd overlap them to give them some strength.

538
00:30:17,821 --> 00:30:19,580
And when they were finally finished,

539
00:30:19,680 --> 00:30:22,460
they would take chains and wrap them around.

540
00:30:22,560 --> 00:30:23,960
And these chains are huge.

541
00:30:24,060 --> 00:30:26,960
These are those anchor chains that you see, absolutely

542
00:30:27,060 --> 00:30:29,360
massive.

543
00:30:29,460 --> 00:30:33,460
The chains alone weighed 250 tons.

544
00:30:33,560 --> 00:30:35,480
Released from their timber frames,

545
00:30:35,580 --> 00:30:39,080
Benson's rafts were as seaworthy as battleships.

546
00:30:39,180 --> 00:30:43,280
Many contained enough lumber to build 500 houses.

547
00:30:43,380 --> 00:30:46,120
Over the next 40 years, close to 120

548
00:30:46,121 --> 00:30:50,960
navigated the 1,100-mile voyage to San Diego.

549
00:30:51,060 --> 00:30:53,760
Innovators like Benson pushed productivity to levels

550
00:30:53,860 --> 00:30:56,260
once thought unimaginable.

551
00:30:56,360 --> 00:30:59,060
Loggers cut a swath through the western timberlands,

552
00:30:59,160 --> 00:31:02,560
as if forests were an inexhaustible resource.

553
00:31:02,660 --> 00:31:06,180
At many sites, they literally stripped the landscape.

554
00:31:06,280 --> 00:31:08,480
Then at the turn of the 20th century,

555
00:31:08,580 --> 00:31:10,980
the shriek of the donkey engine was challenged

556
00:31:11,080 --> 00:31:13,880
by the voice of protest.

557
00:31:13,881 --> 00:31:17,280
Naturalists like John Muir deemed the frontier forests a

558
00:31:17,380 --> 00:31:20,480
living treasure that required protection.

559
00:31:20,580 --> 00:31:24,320
Muir's greatest champion was President Theodore Roosevelt.

560
00:31:24,420 --> 00:31:27,120
As both nature lover and die-hard capitalist,

561
00:31:27,220 --> 00:31:30,360
Roosevelt declared that America had to balance its demand for

562
00:31:30,460 --> 00:31:34,000
lumber with a keener sensitivity to the environment.

563
00:31:34,100 --> 00:31:38,000
In 1905, he created the United States Forest Service

564
00:31:38,100 --> 00:31:40,540
and appointed renowned conservationist Gifford

565
00:31:40,640 --> 00:31:43,000
Pinchot as its head.

566
00:31:43,001 --> 00:31:45,040
Pinchot believed America's national forests

567
00:31:45,140 --> 00:31:48,440
could be managed best by combining a reverence for nature

568
00:31:48,540 --> 00:31:51,400
with responsible exploitation.

569
00:31:51,500 --> 00:31:53,280
The timber barons on the one side

570
00:31:53,380 --> 00:31:57,080
felt that he was too excessive and too restrictive

571
00:31:57,180 --> 00:31:59,480
in terms of how forestry should be practiced.

572
00:31:59,580 --> 00:32:01,180
The John Muirs, on the other hand,

573
00:32:01,280 --> 00:32:04,120
felt that nobody should be using forest, period,

574
00:32:04,220 --> 00:32:07,120
that they should be set aside and left alone.

575
00:32:07,220 --> 00:32:09,560
Gifford Pinchot was a person who said, look,

576
00:32:09,660 --> 00:32:13,000
we can have our cake, and we can eat it too.

577
00:32:13,001 --> 00:32:18,000
Pincho's policy of multiple use set aside some forest land to

578
00:32:18,100 --> 00:32:21,640
be preserved while opening up other regions for lumbermen.

579
00:32:21,740 --> 00:32:25,740
A century after its inception, it still provokes controversy.

580
00:32:27,940 --> 00:32:30,360
Some believe it puts a stranglehold on the logging

581
00:32:30,460 --> 00:32:31,660
industry.

582
00:32:31,760 --> 00:32:35,160
Others think it leaves America's forests too vulnerable.

583
00:32:37,300 --> 00:32:42,200
Beginning in 1926, the debate intensified as the woods echoed

584
00:32:42,201 --> 00:32:44,700
The woods echoed with the roar of a new invention.

585
00:32:51,840 --> 00:32:54,540
The first practical chainsaws were the brainchild

586
00:32:54,541 --> 00:32:58,580
of mechanical engineer Andreas Still.

587
00:32:58,581 --> 00:33:01,260
His key design element was a chain of cutting teeth

588
00:33:01,261 --> 00:33:04,380
that rotated in a groove around a bar.

589
00:33:04,381 --> 00:33:06,480
The blades resembled the simple straight teeth

590
00:33:06,481 --> 00:33:08,660
of a crosscut saw.

591
00:33:08,661 --> 00:33:11,240
Until the 1940s, however, his prototypes

592
00:33:11,241 --> 00:33:15,480
to 150 pounds and required a two-man crew.

593
00:33:15,580 --> 00:33:18,640
The heavy gasoline engines had clutches that had to be engaged

594
00:33:18,740 --> 00:33:20,980
manually.

595
00:33:21,080 --> 00:33:23,880
Later models increased efficiency.

596
00:33:23,980 --> 00:33:28,460
Then in 1946, a logger and inventor named Buford Cox

597
00:33:28,560 --> 00:33:32,000
conceived a radical new design for the sawteeth.

598
00:33:32,100 --> 00:33:34,500
He was sitting at the woodpile one day,

599
00:33:34,600 --> 00:33:37,540
and he was looking at this big wood boring insect.

600
00:33:37,640 --> 00:33:39,800
And they're about as big as your finger.

601
00:33:39,801 --> 00:33:43,240
And what they have is they have big mandible jaws in front.

602
00:33:43,241 --> 00:33:46,100
And they swipe at the timber and the wood

603
00:33:46,101 --> 00:33:49,440
and make this nice oval-shaped hole

604
00:33:49,441 --> 00:33:51,140
that they would bore through.

605
00:33:51,141 --> 00:33:52,740
And he looked at that and he thought,

606
00:33:52,741 --> 00:33:54,540
you know, if they can do that,

607
00:33:54,541 --> 00:33:56,780
I wonder if we could do that with a chainsaw.

608
00:33:56,781 --> 00:33:59,960
What Buford Cox did is he actually took that tooth

609
00:33:59,961 --> 00:34:01,460
and curled it over.

610
00:34:01,461 --> 00:34:05,360
And that corner of that tooth would grab the wood

611
00:34:05,361 --> 00:34:06,500
and pull it out.

612
00:34:06,501 --> 00:34:10,700
And then that really was the start of modern-day chain

613
00:34:10,800 --> 00:34:13,000
for the chainsaws.

614
00:34:13,100 --> 00:34:14,540
The chain became better.

615
00:34:14,640 --> 00:34:16,640
The engines became lighter.

616
00:34:16,740 --> 00:34:19,440
We had better technology in the weight.

617
00:34:19,540 --> 00:34:23,620
We did a better job of the exhaust gases, for example,

618
00:34:23,720 --> 00:34:25,960
moving them away from the operator.

619
00:34:26,060 --> 00:34:28,160
We had the upper hand, if you will,

620
00:34:28,260 --> 00:34:30,260
once the chainsaw came into the woods,

621
00:34:30,360 --> 00:34:34,000
because we could really cut the trees fast.

622
00:34:34,100 --> 00:34:36,260
Today's chainsaws are driven by

623
00:34:36,261 --> 00:34:42,100
a centrifugal clutch and can revolve at up to 13,000

624
00:34:42,200 --> 00:34:45,300
revolutions per minute. As they've revolutionized the

625
00:34:45,400 --> 00:34:49,300
industry over the past 60 years, so has another bold idea from

626
00:34:49,400 --> 00:34:52,400
the 1940s. Before World War II,

627
00:34:52,500 --> 00:34:55,020
foresters began planting seedlings in the barren

628
00:34:55,120 --> 00:34:57,820
landscapes clear cut by lumberjacks.

629
00:34:57,920 --> 00:35:00,660
The new generation of trees was specifically intended to be

630
00:35:00,760 --> 00:35:04,460
harvested decades later as timber.

631
00:35:04,461 --> 00:35:09,460
It marked the birth of the tree farm.

632
00:35:09,560 --> 00:35:13,260
Since then, 37,000 tree farms have sprouted across the United

633
00:35:13,360 --> 00:35:15,700
States, most of them in the South.

634
00:35:18,700 --> 00:35:22,220
Seedlings are first planted and cultivated in nurseries, then

635
00:35:22,320 --> 00:35:25,420
transplanted in harvesting tracts.

636
00:35:25,520 --> 00:35:29,360
They can grow trees much like you grow corn.

637
00:35:29,460 --> 00:35:30,820
Trees are planted in rows.

638
00:35:30,920 --> 00:35:33,920
You can manage them because you can put equipment on the flat

639
00:35:33,921 --> 00:35:39,120
terrain. So there are some advantages to being able to farm

640
00:35:39,220 --> 00:35:44,160
the tree as a crop. Today, more than a third of the

641
00:35:44,260 --> 00:35:48,840
world's harvested timber comes from tree farms. Some experts

642
00:35:48,940 --> 00:35:52,840
predict that by the year 2040, they will cover an expanse the

643
00:35:52,940 --> 00:35:56,640
size of Utah. Their expansion may seem the most

644
00:35:56,740 --> 00:36:00,540
obvious solution to the nation's rising demand for wood, but

645
00:36:00,541 --> 00:36:03,280
But obvious solutions are often not as simple as they first

646
00:36:03,380 --> 00:36:05,180
appear.

647
00:36:05,280 --> 00:36:06,780
A tree farm is not a forest.

648
00:36:06,880 --> 00:36:10,420
A forest is a web of life with many different species,

649
00:36:10,520 --> 00:36:14,580
a great variety of plants, and everything all interacting

650
00:36:14,680 --> 00:36:18,200
together to have literally a web of life.

651
00:36:18,300 --> 00:36:21,460
When you change the forest to a single crop of trees

652
00:36:21,560 --> 00:36:24,800
or even a few species of trees, the result

653
00:36:24,900 --> 00:36:27,640
is a tree plantation that is very sterile.

654
00:36:27,641 --> 00:36:31,340
rather than there being the diversity that is essential

655
00:36:31,440 --> 00:36:36,280
for species to survive in the long term.

656
00:36:36,380 --> 00:36:39,480
Despite such concerns, many see few viable alternatives

657
00:36:39,580 --> 00:36:42,880
as we look to the future.

658
00:36:42,980 --> 00:36:46,760
We're going to go from 300 million people to a billion

659
00:36:46,860 --> 00:36:48,560
people one day in the United States,

660
00:36:48,660 --> 00:36:51,060
and wood has to come from someplace.

661
00:36:51,160 --> 00:36:54,640
So I suspect that we may have to have areas of the United States

662
00:36:54,641 --> 00:36:57,740
though wood fiber has grown on very short rotations

663
00:36:57,840 --> 00:37:02,640
so that we can reduce our costs and yet provide a lot of wood

664
00:37:02,740 --> 00:37:05,580
for America's needs.

665
00:37:05,680 --> 00:37:08,020
The debate is just part of an enduring conflict

666
00:37:08,120 --> 00:37:12,120
between the logging industry and environmentalists.

667
00:37:12,220 --> 00:37:16,100
Their struggle is the result of a society at cross purposes,

668
00:37:16,200 --> 00:37:19,400
one that seeks to protect its resources as much as exploit

669
00:37:19,500 --> 00:37:21,800
them.

670
00:37:21,900 --> 00:37:23,840
In an effort to solve the dilemma,

671
00:37:23,841 --> 00:37:30,240
innovators are literally taking bold new steps.

672
00:37:30,340 --> 00:37:34,380
The 13 southern states produce 60% of America's wood, almost

673
00:37:34,480 --> 00:37:37,180
four times the current volume of the Pacific Northwest.

674
00:37:45,900 --> 00:37:49,300
Today's logging industry faces obstacles Paul Bunyan might have

675
00:37:49,400 --> 00:37:53,840
found insurmountable. It must feed a wood-hungry

676
00:37:53,841 --> 00:37:56,840
that has a passion to preserve its forests.

677
00:37:56,841 --> 00:37:59,840
It must meet the spiraling demands of a new century,

678
00:37:59,841 --> 00:38:03,840
yet avoid the practices that scarred the last.

679
00:38:03,841 --> 00:38:06,840
Three centuries of exploitation have cut deeply into the American

680
00:38:06,841 --> 00:38:10,840
landscape and into the federally owned forests that comprise

681
00:38:10,841 --> 00:38:14,840
29% of the nation's timberlands. In the Sierra Nevada's

682
00:38:14,841 --> 00:38:18,840
National Forest, all the way through until the early 1990s,

683
00:38:18,841 --> 00:38:21,840
clear-cutting was the main method of harvest.

684
00:38:21,841 --> 00:38:24,740
And so what we end up with is a site that

685
00:38:24,840 --> 00:38:29,780
is a bare, denuded hillside, and the values that

686
00:38:29,880 --> 00:38:32,280
are important for wildlife and other species

687
00:38:32,380 --> 00:38:33,780
are no longer there.

688
00:38:33,880 --> 00:38:36,880
It's just putting human needs first, the human needs

689
00:38:36,980 --> 00:38:40,600
of growing a crop of wood.

690
00:38:40,700 --> 00:38:43,600
In their zeal to satisfy the nation's demands,

691
00:38:43,700 --> 00:38:46,440
loggers destroyed much of nature's most monumental

692
00:38:46,540 --> 00:38:48,800
handiwork.

693
00:38:48,801 --> 00:38:53,800
Less than 4% of America's old-growth timber remains.

694
00:38:53,900 --> 00:38:57,200
In the matter of the sequoia trees in Converse Basin

695
00:38:57,300 --> 00:38:59,640
that was logged by the Sanger Lumber Company,

696
00:38:59,740 --> 00:39:01,740
which I call the most destructive logging

697
00:39:01,840 --> 00:39:05,680
in the history of the world, they cut down 8,000 giant sequoias.

698
00:39:05,780 --> 00:39:10,860
And those trees take 2,000, 3,000, 4,000 years to grow big.

699
00:39:10,960 --> 00:39:12,860
Destruction on a scale far greater

700
00:39:12,960 --> 00:39:15,120
is now occurring in South America,

701
00:39:15,121 --> 00:39:19,920
America, where 200,000 square miles of the Amazon rainforest

702
00:39:19,921 --> 00:39:23,760
have fallen. There, few government controls

703
00:39:23,761 --> 00:39:26,960
exist to prevent developers from stripping the landscape of its

704
00:39:26,961 --> 00:39:33,280
rich diversity of wildlife. In the US, loggers operate

705
00:39:33,281 --> 00:39:36,680
according to strict federal and state regulations, but they

706
00:39:36,681 --> 00:39:40,240
continue to cross swords with environmentalists, as each

707
00:39:40,241 --> 00:39:43,280
asserts their own visions of what constitutes responsible

708
00:39:43,281 --> 00:39:44,940
management.

709
00:39:45,040 --> 00:39:47,020
Forest issues are not black and white.

710
00:39:47,120 --> 00:39:50,020
And obviously, the vast majority of foresters

711
00:39:50,120 --> 00:39:53,760
and those trying to produce timber on forest lands

712
00:39:53,860 --> 00:39:57,600
do care about wildlife and water and other values.

713
00:39:57,700 --> 00:39:59,800
And the vast majority of environmentalists

714
00:39:59,900 --> 00:40:02,460
recognize that you have to produce wood products.

715
00:40:02,560 --> 00:40:04,800
So in many ways, the challenge is

716
00:40:04,900 --> 00:40:07,800
for the environmentalists and loggers

717
00:40:07,900 --> 00:40:11,680
to work more and more toward the common middle ground

718
00:40:11,681 --> 00:40:14,680
where there are a lot of overlapping opportunities.

719
00:40:17,680 --> 00:40:20,680
In my grandfather's era after World War II,

720
00:40:20,681 --> 00:40:23,680
it was production, you know.

721
00:40:23,681 --> 00:40:26,680
Get the pieces to the roadside, get them to the mill,

722
00:40:26,681 --> 00:40:29,680
and, you know, brute force and strength.

723
00:40:29,681 --> 00:40:33,680
And today it's more finesse and, you know,

724
00:40:33,681 --> 00:40:36,680
minimal disturbance to the environment.

725
00:40:36,681 --> 00:40:40,680
Today we have to get production, but we have to do it smart.

726
00:40:41,680 --> 00:40:44,920
These days, there's a lot of new high-tech equipment

727
00:40:45,020 --> 00:40:46,920
that's being used in the timber industry.

728
00:40:47,020 --> 00:40:50,680
And our center is very supportive of the conversion

729
00:40:50,780 --> 00:40:52,920
to this high-tech equipment because it benefits

730
00:40:53,020 --> 00:40:54,420
the environment.

731
00:40:54,520 --> 00:40:59,140
The cut-to-length processors have a minimal pressure

732
00:40:59,240 --> 00:41:01,840
per square inch, and so they don't compact the soil

733
00:41:01,940 --> 00:41:03,940
like traditional equipment does.

734
00:41:04,040 --> 00:41:05,840
They don't create huge ruts.

735
00:41:05,940 --> 00:41:09,380
They're very, very light, even on moderately steep hillsides,

736
00:41:09,381 --> 00:41:12,980
very, very light on the land.

737
00:41:13,080 --> 00:41:15,180
The less compacted the soil, the better

738
00:41:15,280 --> 00:41:19,020
the chances for the trees left standing.

739
00:41:19,120 --> 00:41:22,120
Since the 1970s, loggers have demonstrated this most

740
00:41:22,220 --> 00:41:24,920
dramatically with the helicopter.

741
00:41:25,020 --> 00:41:28,260
Nothing protects the fragile forest floor more than rising

742
00:41:28,360 --> 00:41:29,540
above it.

743
00:41:29,640 --> 00:41:33,440
But airborne operations are rare, conducted mainly in remote areas

744
00:41:33,540 --> 00:41:37,240
inaccessible to ground machinery.

745
00:41:37,241 --> 00:41:40,640
Will those types of machines become more important in the

746
00:41:40,740 --> 00:41:42,180
present and into the future?

747
00:41:42,280 --> 00:41:45,680
Probably so, but we're going to have to reduce their cost.

748
00:41:45,780 --> 00:41:47,180
They're very expensive.

749
00:41:47,280 --> 00:41:49,280
I think they're about seven times more expensive

750
00:41:49,380 --> 00:41:50,680
than conventional systems.

751
00:41:53,820 --> 00:41:56,000
Scandinavian engineers are developing what

752
00:41:56,100 --> 00:41:58,100
may be a feasible alternative.

753
00:41:58,200 --> 00:42:02,200
Their six-legged prototypes, aptly called walking machines,

754
00:42:02,300 --> 00:42:05,900
can negotiate even the steepest terrain.

755
00:42:05,901 --> 00:42:09,000
Their impact on productivity could be as profound as their

756
00:42:09,100 --> 00:42:12,700
lack of impact on the forest floor.

757
00:42:12,800 --> 00:42:15,040
That technology is very intriguing.

758
00:42:15,140 --> 00:42:17,640
You're just putting that weight on one spot.

759
00:42:17,740 --> 00:42:21,640
You don't see the impact of like a road through the trees

760
00:42:21,740 --> 00:42:26,760
that you do with using wheeled equipment that creates a path.

761
00:42:26,860 --> 00:42:28,660
It was developed in the Nordic countries

762
00:42:28,760 --> 00:42:32,000
when they were trying to harvest areas that were peat bogs,

763
00:42:32,100 --> 00:42:34,160
and they couldn't hold up the heavy equipment.

764
00:42:34,161 --> 00:42:38,400
equipment, and it senses when it won't have enough support for it

765
00:42:38,500 --> 00:42:40,860
and will put the actual foot of the machine

766
00:42:40,960 --> 00:42:43,600
in a different place once it knows it's not going to support

767
00:42:43,700 --> 00:42:46,300
it.

768
00:42:46,400 --> 00:42:49,000
Can technology help bridge the gap between the logging

769
00:42:49,100 --> 00:42:51,480
industry and environmentalists?

770
00:42:51,580 --> 00:42:55,120
Both camps are hopeful, but their enduring struggle seems

771
00:42:55,220 --> 00:42:58,460
destined to escalate at the same rate as our global appetite

772
00:42:58,560 --> 00:43:00,820
for wood.

773
00:43:00,920 --> 00:43:04,060
Policymakers will have to balance our needs as consumers

774
00:43:04,160 --> 00:43:07,660
with our concerns for the health of our forests.

775
00:43:07,760 --> 00:43:10,400
Right now, it's at a stalemate.

776
00:43:10,500 --> 00:43:12,900
And until we have some charismatic leaders

777
00:43:13,000 --> 00:43:15,500
come forward from the environmental community

778
00:43:15,600 --> 00:43:18,880
and from the timber industry and the agencies that

779
00:43:18,980 --> 00:43:20,920
manage our public lands, we're not

780
00:43:21,020 --> 00:43:23,520
going to have a resolution of the conflict.

781
00:43:23,620 --> 00:43:25,520
Most environmentalists recognize

782
00:43:25,620 --> 00:43:28,460
that we all need to use wood, and they don't want to say,

783
00:43:28,560 --> 00:43:31,660
stop logging, even though there will be a few that

784
00:43:31,661 --> 00:43:34,320
that have that extreme view.

785
00:43:34,480 --> 00:43:36,700
But the vast majority of environmental groups

786
00:43:36,701 --> 00:43:40,300
recognize that responsible forest management

787
00:43:40,301 --> 00:43:42,820
can provide wood products that we all need.

788
00:43:45,960 --> 00:43:48,020
We need to be able to go to the lumber yard

789
00:43:48,021 --> 00:43:49,020
and buy two by four.

790
00:43:49,120 --> 00:43:50,460
We need to be able to put up houses.

791
00:43:51,240 --> 00:43:53,920
But we don't need to clear cut the way we used to.

792
00:43:54,040 --> 00:43:56,120
We don't need to destroy the wilderness

793
00:43:56,121 --> 00:43:57,260
the way we used to.

794
00:43:57,640 --> 00:43:59,280
So we need some moderation.

795
00:43:59,620 --> 00:44:00,820
We need government control.

796
00:44:00,821 --> 00:44:04,260
John Muir said that Uncle Sam was the only one who could preserve

797
00:44:04,360 --> 00:44:07,260
the forest, and he was absolutely right.

798
00:44:07,360 --> 00:44:11,260
If the past is prologue, loggers will continue

799
00:44:11,360 --> 00:44:13,560
to meet our insatiable demand for wood

800
00:44:13,660 --> 00:44:16,160
with imagination and innovation.

801
00:44:16,260 --> 00:44:18,980
In a complex world that strives to revere nature

802
00:44:19,080 --> 00:44:21,180
as well as exploit it, their challenge

803
00:44:21,280 --> 00:44:24,580
will be to defy the popular saying

804
00:44:24,680 --> 00:44:27,280
and always see the forest for the trees.

